## Setup

Let the 29 containers be the set $[29]=\{1,\dots,29\}$. A "manifest" is a 5-element subset. Assigning every manifest to Zone Alpha or Zone Beta is a **2-coloring** of all 5-subsets of $[29]$. A collection of pairwise disjoint manifests all lying in one zone is a **monochromatic matching** (a family of pairwise vertex-disjoint 5-edges of a single color) in the complete 5-uniform hypergraph $K^{(5)}_{29}$. We seek the largest $k$ such that *every* 2-coloring of the 5-subsets of $[29]$ contains a monochromatic matching of size $k$.

## Upper bound: $k\le 5$

A matching of size $k$ consists of $k$ pairwise disjoint 5-sets and therefore uses $5k$ distinct containers. Since $5k\le 29$ forces $k\le\lfloor 29/5\rfloor=5$, no matching of size $6$ can exist in $[29]$ (it would require $30$ containers). Hence $k\le 5$.

## Lower bound: $k\ge 5$

We use the Cockayne–Lorimer theorem on multi-color Ramsey numbers for matchings in uniform hypergraphs (Cockayne & Lorimer, 1975):

> **Theorem (Cockayne–Lorimer, 2 colors).** For $r\ge 2$ and $n_1\ge n_2\ge 1$,
> $$R\!\bigl(n_1 K_r^{(r)},\, n_2 K_r^{(r)}\bigr)\;=\; n_1\,r + n_2 - 1,$$
> i.e. every red/blue coloring of the $r$-subsets of an $(n_1 r+n_2-1)$-element set contains either a red matching of size $n_1$ or a blue matching of size $n_2$, and the bound is tight.

**Verification of the formula on checkable cases.**

- *Graph case $r=2$.* The formula gives $R(nK_2,mK_2)=2n+m-1$ ($n\ge m$), the classical multicolor matching Ramsey number for graphs. For instance $R(2K_2,2K_2)=5$: $K_4$ can be 2-colored with no monochromatic 2-matching (color a triangle red and the complementary star blue — each color class is an intersecting family), while in $K_5$ each color class must be an intersecting family of size $\le 4$ (a star), so the two classes cover at most $8<10=\binom{5}{2}$ edges — contradiction. ✓

- *3-uniform case $r=3$, $n_1=n_2=2$.* The formula gives $R=3\cdot2+2-1=7$. Indeed $[6]$ is colorable with no monochromatic pair of disjoint triples (take $A$ of size $5$, $B$ of size $1$; color triples inside $A$ red, triples meeting $B$ blue — red matching $\le 1$ since $|A|=5<6$, blue matching $\le 1$ since $|B|=1$). For $[7]$: if neither color has two disjoint triples, each color class is an intersecting family of triples on $7$ points; by Erdős–Ko–Rado each has size $\le\binom{6}{2}=15$, so together they cover $\le 30<\binom{7}{3}=35$ triples — contradiction. ✓

**Tightness (lower-bound construction for the theorem).** For $n_1\ge n_2$, take disjoint sets $A$ (size $n_1 r-1$) and $B$ (size $n_2-1$), total $n_1 r+n_2-2$. Color an $r$-subset **red** if it lies entirely in $A$, **blue** otherwise (i.e. if it meets $B$). Red matchings use only vertices of $A$, of which there are $n_1 r-1<n_1 r$, so every red matching has size $\le n_1-1$. Blue matchings: each blue edge contains a vertex of $B$, and disjoint blue edges contain *distinct* vertices of $B$, so every blue matching has size $\le |B|=n_2-1$. Thus $[n_1 r+n_2-2]$ admits a coloring with no monochromatic matching of the target sizes, confirming $R\ge n_1 r+n_2-1$. The theorem supplies the matching upper bound.

**Application.** With $r=5$ and $n_1=n_2=5$:
$$R\!\bigl(5K_5^{(5)},\,5K_5^{(5)}\bigr)=5\cdot 5+5-1=29.$$
Since $29\le 29$, **every** red/blue coloring of the 5-subsets of a 29-element set contains a monochromatic matching of size $5$. Translating back: no matter how the manifests are split between Zone Alpha and Zone Beta, one zone always contains at least $5$ pairwise disjoint manifests. Hence $k\ge 5$.

(For completeness, the construction above with $n_1=n_2=5$, $r=5$ shows that on $28=5\cdot5+5-2$ containers one can 2-color all manifests so that *neither* zone has $5$ disjoint manifests — confirming the bound $29$ is sharp.)

## Conclusion

Combining $k\le 5$ (disjointness ceiling) and $k\ge 5$ (Cockayne–Lorimer),

$$\boxed{k=5}.$$

